### 1. Internal `Node<E>` Structure

Unlike `ArrayList` which stores elements inside a single contiguous array, `LinkedList` allocates an independent object in the heap for every single element:
```java
private static class Node<E> {
    E item;          // The payload element
    Node<E> next;    // Reference to following node
    Node<E> prev;    // Reference to preceding node

    Node(Node<E> prev, E element, Node<E> next) {
        this.item = element;
        this.next = next;
        this.prev = prev;
    }
}
```

```text
Visual Memory Representation of Doubly-Linked List:

 [first]                                              [last]
    |                                                    |
    v                                                    v
+--------+      +--------+      +--------+      +--------+
| null   | <--- | prev   | <--- | prev   | <--- | prev   |
| Node A | ---> | Node B | ---> | Node C | ---> | Node D | ---> null
+--------+      +--------+      +--------+      +--------+
```

### 2. The Index Lookup Optimization

When you call `linkedList.get(index)`, how does Java find the node? It cannot do array pointer math because nodes are scattered unpredictably across heap RAM.

Java checks whether the requested index is closer to the beginning or the end:
```java
Node<E> node(int index) {
    // If index is in the first half of the list, walk forward from head
    if (index < (size >> 1)) {
        Node<E> x = first;
        for (int i = 0; i < index; i++)
            x = x.next;
        return x;
    } else {
        // If index is in the second half, walk backward from tail
        Node<E> x = last;
        for (int i = size - 1; i > index; i--)
            x = x.prev;
        return x;
    }
}
```
> **Critical Insight**: Even though this cuts the average number of node traversals in half ($n/4$ hops on average), its asymptotic time complexity is still **$O(n)$ linear time**!

### 3. Dual Personality: `List` and `Deque`

`LinkedList` implements both interfaces:
```java
public class LinkedList<E> extends AbstractSequentialList<E>
    implements List<E>, Deque<E>, Cloneable, java.io.Serializable
```

Because it implements `Deque<E>`, it provides true $O(1)$ operations at both ends:
- `addFirst(E e)` / `offerFirst(E e)`
- `addLast(E e)` / `offerLast(E e)`
- `removeFirst()` / `pollFirst()`
- `removeLast()` / `pollLast()`
- `peekFirst()` / `peekLast()`

### 4. The Hidden Memory Tax of LinkedList

On a 64-bit HotSpot JVM (with compressed OOPs disabled or heap > 32GB):
- Each `Node` object header: 16 bytes
- `item` reference: 8 bytes
- `next` pointer: 8 bytes
- `prev` pointer: 8 bytes
- Total: **40 bytes per node!** (Plus the size of the payload object itself!)

In contrast, an `ArrayList` stores a single reference in its array (4–8 bytes per slot). Storing 1,000,000 integers in a `LinkedList` consumes ~48 MB of RAM, compared to ~8 MB in an `ArrayList`!

